    gospelText: `Matthew 15:21-28 (NABRE, Lectionary for Mass) — Jesus withdraws to the region of Tyre and Sidon, Gentile territory. A Canaanite woman of that district comes out and cries after him, "Have pity on me, Lord, Son of David! My daughter is tormented by a demon." Jesus does not say a word in answer. The disciples ask him to send her away, "for she keeps calling out after us." Jesus replies, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel." But the woman comes and does him homage, saying simply, "Lord, help me." He answers, "It is not right to take the food of the children and throw it to the dogs." She responds, "Please, Lord, for even the dogs eat the scraps that fall from the table of their masters." Then Jesus says to her, "O woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you wish" — and from that hour her daughter is healed.`,
    firstReading: `Isaiah 56:1, 6-7 — The opening of Third Isaiah widens the covenant's doors: "Observe what is right, do what is just; for my salvation is about to come." The Lord speaks of "the foreigners who join themselves to the Lord, ministering to him, loving the name of the Lord" — them, too, he will bring to his holy mountain "and make joyful in my house of prayer." Their offerings will be acceptable on his altar, "for my house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples." The verse Jesus will quote when he cleanses the Temple is here in its home context: a promise that the God of Israel intends his house, his altar, and his joy for the nations — the charter of the Canaanite woman's hope.`,
    psalm: `Psalm 67:2-3, 5, 6, 8 — "O God, let all the nations praise you!" The psalm turns Aaron's priestly blessing outward: may God's face shine upon us "so may your way be known upon earth; among all nations, your salvation." The nations are summoned to gladness — "may the nations be glad and exult" — and the psalm ends with the harvest logic of election: God blesses Israel so that "all the ends of the earth may fear him." Israel is blessed for the world's sake; the Canaanite woman stands among the nations the psalm has been expecting all along.`,
    secondReading: `Romans 11:13-15, 29-32 — Paul, "the apostle to the Gentiles," glories in his ministry in hope of making his own people jealous and so saving some of them; if their rejection brought reconciliation to the world, their acceptance will be nothing less than "life from the dead." Then the sentence on which the Church's whole theology of Israel rests: "For the gifts and the call of God are irrevocable." The Gentiles, once disobedient, "have now received mercy"; Israel's present disobedience will likewise end in mercy, "for God delivered all to disobedience, that he might have mercy upon all." Mercy is the last word of the whole argument — the same mercy the Canaanite woman begged as "scraps" and received as a feast.`,
    context: `On the 20th Sunday of Ordinary Time the lectionary takes a decisive geographical and theological step: Jesus withdraws "to the region of Tyre and Sidon" — outside the Holy Land, into pagan Phoenicia. The journey follows a bruising dispute with the Pharisees and scribes over ritual purity (Matthew 15:1-20), in which Jesus declared that it is not what enters the mouth that defiles. The sequence is deliberate: having dismantled the purity boundary in principle, Jesus now crosses it in person, and the first human being he meets is a triple outsider — a woman, a pagan, a Canaanite.

Matthew's word choice is archaic on purpose. Mark calls her "a Greek, a Syrophoenician by birth" (Mark 7:26) — the current ethnic label; Matthew reaches back a millennium and calls her Χαναναία (*Chananaia*), "a Canaanite" — the name of Israel's ancient enemies, the dispossessed peoples of the conquest, a word soaked in old hostility. Out of this most unlikely mouth comes the most orthodox address in the region: "Have pity on me, Lord, Son of David!" — Κύριε, υἱὸς Δαυίδ. A Canaanite confesses Israel's Messiah with a title most of Israel's own leadership refuses him.

Then comes the strangest passage in the Gospels: Jesus is silent; then apparently refuses ("I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel"); then speaks the hard word about the children's bread and the dogs. The Greek softens what English flattens: κυνάρια (*kynaria*) is a diminutive — the house-dogs, the puppies under the family table, not the scavenging street dogs — and the woman seizes precisely this opening. Her reply turns the image inside out without contradicting it: "even the dogs eat the scraps that fall from the table of their masters." She does not dispute Israel's priority — she locates herself inside the household under its table, close enough for crumbs. It is the only time in the Gospels that anyone "wins" an exchange with Jesus, and he crowns her for it: μεγάλη σου ἡ πίστις — "great is your faith" — an accolade whose only parallel in Matthew is another Gentile, the centurion of Capernaum (8:10).

The Fathers wrestled honestly with Jesus' silence and severity, and their common answer remains the best: the delay was not refusal but revelation. Chrysostom says Jesus withheld the answer "that he might display the woman's treasure" — a faith that silence could not discourage, rebuff could not offend, and wit could not fail. Jesus does not change his mind in the story; he draws out, step by step, for the disciples who wanted her dismissed, the spectacle of a faith greater than any they had yet managed — the same disciples recently labeled "you of little faith."

Placed between Isaiah 56 ("a house of prayer for all peoples") and Romans 11 ("God has mercy upon all"), the episode reads as the hinge of salvation history in miniature: mission to Israel first — a priority Jesus states and Paul defends — opening, without being revoked, into mercy for the nations. Two chapters later in Matthew's narrative arc, Jesus will feed four thousand on the Gentile side of the sea; at the Gospel's end he will send the Eleven to "all nations."

On authorship and date, mainstream Catholic scholarship places Matthew around AD 80–90, writing for a Jewish-Christian church learning to receive Gentiles. Daniel J. Harrington, SJ (*The Gospel of Matthew*, Sacra Pagina 1, pp. 234–239) reads the pericope as Matthew's community working out the order of the mission — Israel first, then the nations — with the woman as proof that Gentile faith was no dilution. Pope Benedict XVI, in *Jesus of Nazareth*, treats Jesus' restriction to Israel as the necessary first act of a universal drama: the promises had to be fulfilled within Israel in order to be opened from Israel to the world.`,
