    gospelText: `Matthew 14:22-33 (NABRE, Lectionary for Mass) — After feeding the crowds, Jesus makes the disciples get into a boat and precede him across the sea while he dismisses the people; then he goes up the mountain alone to pray, and remains there into the night. The boat, meanwhile, is "a few miles offshore," tossed by waves, "for the wind was against it." In the fourth watch of the night Jesus comes toward them, walking on the sea. The terrified disciples cry out, "It is a ghost!" But at once Jesus speaks: "Take courage, it is I; do not be afraid." Peter answers, "Lord, if it is you, command me to come to you on the water." Jesus says, "Come." Peter gets out of the boat and walks on the water toward Jesus — but seeing how strong the wind is, he becomes frightened, begins to sink, and cries out, "Lord, save me!" Immediately Jesus stretches out his hand, catches him, and says, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?" When they climb into the boat, the wind dies down, and those in the boat do him homage, saying, "Truly, you are the Son of God."`,
    firstReading: `1 Kings 19:9a, 11-13a — Elijah, fleeing Jezebel in despair, comes to the mountain of God at Horeb and shelters in a cave. Told to stand on the mountain, he witnesses a wind "so strong that it was crushing rocks" — "but the Lord was not in the wind." An earthquake follows — "but the Lord was not in the earthquake" — then fire — "but the Lord was not in the fire." After the fire comes "a tiny whispering sound," and hearing it, Elijah hides his face in his cloak and goes to stand at the entrance of the cave. The God whom the prophet expected in spectacle arrives in near-silence — the same God who, on another storm-swept night, will come to his disciples not in the wind but with a quiet word: "It is I."`,
    psalm: `Psalm 85:9-10, 11-12, 13-14 — "Lord, let us see your kindness, and grant us your salvation." The psalmist listens for what God speaks — "for he proclaims peace to his people" — and sees a vision of restored creation in which "kindness and truth shall meet; justice and peace shall kiss," truth springing from the earth while justice looks down from heaven. It is the psalm of a people straining, like Elijah at the cave mouth and the disciples in the boat, to hear the voice of peace above the noise of their circumstances.`,
    secondReading: `Romans 9:1-5 — Paul turns from the triumph of Romans 8 to the anguish of Israel's unbelief, and swears to the depth of it: "I have great sorrow and constant anguish in my heart." He could wish himself "accursed and cut off from Christ for the sake of my own people," his kindred according to the flesh: Israelites, to whom belong "the adoption, the glory, the covenants, the giving of the law, the worship, and the promises," the patriarchs — and from whom, according to the flesh, is the Christ, "who is over all, God blessed forever." Paul's storm is interior: a love for his people that costs him anguish, held together with unshaken confession of the Messiah who has come from them.`,
    context: `The 19th Sunday of Ordinary Time continues directly from last week's Gospel: the walking on the water follows the feeding of the five thousand in a single day-and-night sequence. Matthew notes that Jesus "made" the disciples get into the boat — ἠνάγκασεν (*ēnankasen*), literally "compelled" — while he dismissed the crowds himself. John's parallel explains the urgency: after the feeding, the crowd wanted to carry Jesus off and make him king (John 6:15). The disciples are sent out of temptation's reach, and Jesus at last claims the solitude he was denied at the start of the day: he goes "up on the mountain by himself to pray," and evening finds him there alone.

Matthew then cuts between two scenes: Christ on the mountain in prayer, and the boat "a few miles offshore… tossed about by the waves, for the wind was against it." The Fathers read the contrast as an image of the Church's whole condition in history — the Lord interceding on high, his people laboring against contrary winds below — and Matthew's first hearers, a persecuted community in the decades after the Temple's fall, would hardly have needed the allegory explained. The "fourth watch of the night" (between 3 and 6 a.m., by Roman reckoning) means the disciples have been rowing against the storm most of the night before help comes: the delay belongs to the story's meaning.

The center of the passage is a theophany, and Matthew builds it from Old Testament materials. In Job, God alone "treads upon the crests of the sea" (Job 9:8); in the Exodus psalm, "through the sea was your way… though your footsteps were not seen" (Psalm 77:20). The sea in Israel's imagination is the remnant of chaos, subdued at creation and held back by God's command; to walk on it is a divine prerogative, not a human achievement. And Jesus' word of reassurance carries the heaviest freight in the passage: θαρσεῖτε, ἐγώ εἰμι (*tharseite, egō eimi*) — "Take courage, it is I" — where ἐγώ εἰμι, "I AM," is the Septuagint's rendering of the divine name revealed at the burning bush (Exodus 3:14) and echoed through Isaiah ("I am he… fear not"). On the surface, an ordinary self-identification; underneath, the name of God spoken over the waters of chaos.

The episode of Peter — only in Matthew — turns the theophany into a story of discipleship. Peter's request ("command me to come to you on the water"), his walking, his fear at the wind, his sinking, his three-word prayer (Κύριε, σῶσόν με — "Lord, save me!"), and the immediate grasp of Jesus' hand form a miniature of the life of faith, and of Peter's own arc in this Gospel: bold confession followed by failure followed by rescue. Jesus' gentle rebuke coins a word Matthew loves: ὀλιγόπιστε (*oligopiste*), "you of little faith" — not faithless, but of faith too small for the wind it faced.

The scene ends where Matthew's Gospel is heading: "those in the boat did him homage" — προσεκύνησαν (*prosekynēsan*), the verb of worship — "saying, 'Truly, you are the Son of God.'" It is the first time in Matthew that the disciples as a group confess the divine sonship, anticipating Peter at Caesarea Philippi and the centurion at the cross.

On authorship and date, mainstream Catholic scholarship places Matthew around AD 80–90. Daniel J. Harrington, SJ (*The Gospel of Matthew*, Sacra Pagina 1, pp. 223–228) underlines the ecclesial cast of Matthew's redaction: the boat, the storm, the little-faith disciple, and the worshiping community are the Church's self-portrait. Pope Benedict XVI, in *Jesus of Nazareth*, treats the walking on the water among the scenes in which the disciples encounter Jesus' hidden divinity and are moved to worship — the ἐγώ εἰμι on the sea belonging with the burning bush as a self-disclosure of the living God.`,
