    usccbReadingsUrl: 'https://bible.usccb.org/bible/readings/071926.cfm',
    gospelText: `Matthew 13:24-43 (NABRE, Lectionary for Mass) — Jesus proposes another parable to the crowds: the kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed "good seed in his field," but while everyone was asleep his enemy came and "sowed weeds all through the wheat." When the crop grew and bore fruit, the weeds appeared as well. The householder's slaves ask where the weeds came from, and he answers, "An enemy has done this." When they offer to pull the weeds up, he says no, "if you pull up the weeds you might uproot the wheat along with them. Let them grow together until harvest"; then the harvesters will collect the weeds first for burning and "gather the wheat into my barn." Jesus adds two more parables: the kingdom is like "a mustard seed" — "the smallest of all the seeds," yet when full-grown "the largest of plants," a bush in whose branches "the birds of the sky come and dwell" — and like yeast that a woman mixed with "three measures of wheat flour until the whole batch was leavened." Matthew notes that Jesus spoke to the crowds only in parables, to fulfill the prophet's word: "I will open my mouth in parables, I will announce what has lain hidden from the foundation of the world." Then, dismissing the crowds and entering the house, Jesus explains the weeds at the disciples' request: the sower of good seed is "the Son of Man," the field is the world, the good seed "the children of the kingdom," the weeds "the children of the evil one," the enemy the devil, the harvest "the end of the age," and the harvesters angels. At the end of the age the Son of Man will send his angels to collect out of his kingdom "all who cause others to sin and all evildoers," while "the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears ought to hear."`,
    firstReading: `Wisdom 12:13, 16-19 — Addressed to the God "who have the care of all," the sage declares that God's "mastery over all things makes you lenient to all." His might is "the source of justice," and because he is master of might, he judges "with clemency" and governs "with much lenience." In doing so he has taught his people a lesson the Gospel parable will dramatize: "those who are just must be kind," and he has given his children "good ground for hope that you would permit repentance for their sins." Divine power shows itself not in swift destruction but in patience that leaves room for conversion — exactly the householder's "let them grow together until harvest."`,
    psalm: `Psalm 86:5-6, 9-10, 15-16 — A prayer of trust in the God of patience: "Lord, you are good and forgiving, abounding in kindness to all who call upon you." The psalmist begs God to hearken to his prayer, foresees that "all the nations you have made shall come and worship you," and confesses the ancient creed of Sinai: "You, O Lord, are a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in kindness and fidelity." The refrain — "Lord, you are good and forgiving" — sets the parable of the weeds within the mercy of the God who is slow to anger.`,
    secondReading: `Romans 8:26-27 — Continuing last Sunday's reading on creation groaning toward glory, Paul turns to prayer: "the Spirit too comes to the aid of our weakness; for we do not know how to pray as we ought, but the Spirit itself intercedes with inexpressible groanings." And "the one who searches hearts knows what is the intention of the Spirit, because it intercedes for the holy ones according to God's will." In the mixed field of the present age, where we cannot even see clearly enough to pray rightly, the Spirit himself prays within us — the interior counterpart of the householder's patience without.`,
    context: `The 16th Sunday of Ordinary Time continues the lectionary's three-week reading of Matthew 13, the "Parable Discourse" that stands at the structural center of the Gospel. Last Sunday gave the parable of the sower and its interpretation; this Sunday gives the next three parables — the weeds among the wheat, the mustard seed, and the leaven — together with Jesus' explanation of the weeds. Next Sunday will complete the chapter with the treasure, the pearl, and the net.

The parable of the weeds is unique to Matthew, and it comes with a deliberate change of scene at its center. Jesus speaks the three parables publicly, from the boat, to "the crowds"; then he "dismissed the crowds and went into the house" (13:36), where "his disciples approached him" for the interpretation. The movement from shore to house, from crowd to disciples, enacts the very division the discourse has been describing: the parable is spoken to all, but its meaning is unfolded to those who follow him inside.

The Greek is precise and vivid. The "weeds" are ζιζάνια (*zizania*) — darnel (*Lolium temulentum*), a poisonous rye-grass that in its early growth is nearly indistinguishable from wheat and whose roots intertwine with the wheat's own, so that the householder's caution is sound agronomy before it is theology: pulling the one really would uproot the other. The decisive command is ἄφετε συναυξάνεσθαι ἀμφότερα (*aphete synauxanesthai amphotera*), "let them both grow together" (v. 30) — and the verb ἀφίημι (*aphiēmi*), "let, allow," is the same verb the New Testament uses for "forgive." The patience of the householder is not negligence but a kind of forbearance. The harvest, θερισμός (*therismos*), is a standard prophetic image for the final judgment (cf. Joel 4:13), and Jesus' interpretation names its moment with the solemn phrase συντέλεια τοῦ αἰῶνος (*synteleia tou aiōnos*), "the end of the age" (vv. 39-40).

The two smaller parables answer a different anxiety — not the presence of evil in the kingdom's field, but the apparent insignificance of the kingdom's beginnings. The mustard seed, proverbially "the smallest of all the seeds," becomes a bush large enough that "the birds of the sky come and dwell in its branches," an image drawn from Ezekiel 17:23 and Daniel 4:9-18, where the great tree sheltering the birds is a kingdom sheltering the nations. The leaven (ζύμη, *zymē*) works hidden inside "three measures of wheat flour" — roughly fifty kilograms, enough bread for a hundred people, and the same festive quantity Sarah kneaded for the three heavenly visitors at Mamre (Genesis 18:6). Smallness and hiddenness, both parables insist, are not signs of the kingdom's failure but its method.

Matthew closes the public teaching with a fulfillment citation from Psalm 78:2 — "I will open my mouth in parables, I will announce what has lain hidden from the foundation of the world" — presenting Jesus' parables not as mere pedagogy but as revelation of God's hidden design. The interpretation of the weeds then ends with a promise drawn from Daniel 12:3: "the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father."

On authorship and date, mainstream Catholic scholarship places the Gospel of Matthew around AD 80–90, composed in Greek by a Jewish-Christian author for a mixed community that knew from experience that the Church herself is a field of wheat and weeds. Daniel J. Harrington, SJ (*The Gospel of Matthew*, Sacra Pagina 1, pp. 204–212) notes that the parable and its interpretation address a pastoral question alive in Matthew's community — why the Son of Man's kingdom still contains "all who cause others to sin" — and counsel patience grounded in the certainty of God's final sorting. Pope Benedict XVI treats the parables of chapter 13 in *Jesus of Nazareth* (vol. 1, ch. 7), reading them as figures of the hidden, non-coercive way God's reign enters history.`,
